<div>
    <p>Tearing down everything to find what cannot fall</p>
      <img src="/phil1301/assets/images/henry-fuseli-featured-e1728132654150.png" alt="Henry Fuseli, The Nightmare, 1781. A sleeping figure lies draped across a bed while a demon crouches on her chest and a wild-eyed horse emerges from the darkness behind a curtain." width="860" height="300" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"> 
    <div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
        <p>Read this page alongside or after Meditation I. It unpacks the three doubt-inducing arguments, explains what Descartes is trying to accomplish, and introduces the logic of the foundationalist project.</p>
    </div></div>
    
    <div class="tabs"><div class="tabs__bar" role="tablist"><button class="tabs__tab tabs__tab--active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-0" id="tabs-0-tab-0">▸ 1 &nbsp;—&nbsp; What Descartes is doing and why</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-1" id="tabs-0-tab-1">▸ 2 &nbsp;—&nbsp; The three arguments for doubt</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-2" id="tabs-0-tab-2">▸ 3 &nbsp;—&nbsp; What doubt accomplishes</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-3" id="tabs-0-tab-3">▸ 4 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Common questions and objections</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-4" id="tabs-0-tab-4">▸ 5 &nbsp;—&nbsp; What to watch for in Meditation II</button></div><div class="tabs__panel tabs__panel--active" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-0" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-0"><div>
            <p>Meditation I opens with Descartes sitting alone, by a fire, resolved to do something he has been putting off for years: examine everything he believes and throw out anything that could possibly be false. His goal is to find beliefs so secure that doubt cannot touch them — and to use those beliefs as the foundation for rebuilding his entire understanding of the world.</p>
            <p>He is explicit that he does not intend to examine each belief individually — there are too many. Instead, he looks for the underlying <em>principles</em> on which his beliefs rest. If he can undermine those, everything built on them comes down at once. His main target in Meditation I is sensory experience: almost everything he believes about the external world has come through the senses. If the senses can be shown to be unreliable — even in principle — that is enough to put all of it in doubt.</p>
            <p>This is an unusual standard. In everyday life, we do not demand certainty — we settle for good enough. Descartes is not doing everyday epistemology. He is asking what the foundation of knowledge looks like when you refuse to settle for anything less than the absolutely certain. That project requires something stronger than ordinary skepticism. It requires <strong>hyperbolic doubt</strong>: doubt pushed to the maximum, beyond anything practically motivated, until only the indestructible remains.</p>
            
            <div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>The standard Descartes sets</p>
                <p>Descartes does not ask: <em>is this belief probably true?</em> He asks: <em>is it even conceivable that this belief is false?</em> Any belief that fails that test — even a belief we would never question in practice — gets set aside. Keep this standard in mind as you follow his three arguments. Each one widens the circle of doubt.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-1" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-1" hidden=""><div>
            <p>Descartes builds his case for doubt in three stages, each one more radical than the last. Notice that each argument is designed to undermine a larger category of belief than the previous one.</p>
            <h3>First argument: the senses sometimes deceive</h3>
            <p>Descartes begins with the observation that the senses have deceived him before. Objects look different at a distance. A stick in water appears bent. Colors vary in different light. If the senses have been wrong in the past, prudence suggests not trusting them completely — a source that has deceived you once might deceive you again.</p>
            <p>Descartes quickly notes the limits of this argument, however. It only undermines beliefs about things that are small, distant, or otherwise hard to perceive clearly. He is sitting by a fire right now, holding paper in his hands. Surely the senses are reliable about <em>that</em>. He needs something stronger.</p>
            <h3>Second argument: the Dream Argument</h3>
            <p>How does he know he is not dreaming right now? He has had vivid dreams before in which he seemed to be sitting by a fire just like this one — and only waking revealed them to be dreams. There is no test he can perform, from inside the experience, that would definitively rule out the possibility of dreaming. If that is true, then the mere fact that an experience seems vivid and coherent is not enough to establish that it is real.</p>
            <p>This is a genuinely unsettling argument. It does not just cast doubt on distant or ambiguous perceptions. It casts doubt on <em>all</em> perceptual experience — including the immediate, the vivid, and the seemingly obvious. Descartes can no longer be certain that there is a fire, that he has hands, or that the external world exists at all.</p>
            <p>But notice what the Dream Argument does not touch: even in dreams, mathematical truths hold. Two plus two equals four whether he is awake or asleep. Something seems to survive doubt so far.</p>
            <h3>Third argument: the Evil Demon hypothesis</h3>
            <p>Descartes’ most radical move is the hypothesis of an <strong>evil demon</strong> (<em>malin génie</em>) — a supremely powerful being whose sole purpose is to deceive him in every possible way. Not merely a God who sometimes permits error, but a being dedicated entirely to illusion. If such a demon existed, it could feed him false experiences about the physical world, but also false beliefs about mathematics, about logic, about everything he takes to be obvious and self-evident.</p>
            <p>This is not a claim that such a demon exists. Descartes does not believe it does. The Evil Demon is a thought experiment — a device for making doubt as comprehensive as possible. Its purpose is to show that even the most apparently secure beliefs are, in principle, susceptible to doubt. By the end of Meditation I, Descartes has talked himself into a position of radical uncertainty about nearly everything he has ever believed.</p>
            
            <div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Logic in action: modus tollens</p>
                <p>Each of Descartes’ doubt arguments follows a similar logical pattern. Take the Dream Argument:</p>
                <p><strong>P1.</strong> If I can know something through perception, then I can always tell whether I’m dreaming or awake.</p>
                <p><strong>P2.</strong> I cannot always tell whether I’m dreaming or awake.</p>
                <p><strong>C.</strong> Therefore, I cannot know things through perception alone.</p>
                <p>This form — <em>if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P</em> — is called <strong>modus tollens</strong>. It is one of the most common and reliable argument forms in philosophy. Notice that the conclusion only follows if you accept both premises. Descartes’ critics often attack P1. Is the premise fair? See the <strong>Logic Reference</strong> page for the full definition.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-2" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-2" hidden=""><div>
            <p>It is worth pausing to ask: what has Descartes actually established by the end of Meditation I? He has not proven that the world does not exist, or that mathematics is unreliable, or that an evil demon is at work. He has shown only that these things cannot be <em>ruled out</em> by the resources currently available to him. That is a much more limited claim — but for his purposes, it is enough.</p>
            <p>What the doubt accomplishes is <em>methodological</em>: it clears the ground. Every belief that could conceivably be false gets suspended. What remains — if anything remains — will be exactly what Descartes was looking for: a belief so certain that not even the most comprehensive doubt can shake it.</p>
            <p>Notice also what Descartes has <em>not</em> done: he has not claimed that doubt is his permanent position. He is not a skeptic in the sense of someone who denies the possibility of knowledge. He is using doubt as a tool. The goal of the <em>Meditations</em> is to defeat skepticism, not to embrace it. This distinction matters: Descartes is a foundationalist, not a skeptic, and the difference will become clear in Meditation II.</p>
            
            <div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Logic concept: foundationalism</p>
                <p>A <strong>foundationalist</strong> holds that all justified belief ultimately rests on a set of basic beliefs that require no further justification — they are self-evident, incorrigible, or indubitable. Descartes’ project is foundationalist in structure: he is systematically demolishing beliefs in search of the ones that survive demolition. Whatever survives will serve as the foundation from which everything else is rebuilt. The structure of the <em>Meditations</em> as a whole is deductive in the way a geometric proof is: each step follows from the last. See the <strong>Logic Reference</strong> page for more on deductive arguments and valid inference.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-3" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-3" hidden=""><div>
            <h3>“This is ridiculous — obviously I’m not dreaming.”</h3>
            <p>This is the most common reaction to Meditation I, and it is worth taking seriously rather than dismissing. Descartes would agree that it is obvious you are not dreaming. His point is not that you probably are. His point is that <em>obvious</em> is not the same as <em>certain</em>. If your standard for knowledge is certainty — the kind of certainty that survives all possible doubt — then the mere fact that waking life seems obvious and coherent is not enough. Can you produce an argument that rules out dreaming, rather than just an appeal to how things seem?</p>
            <h3>“Why should certainty be the standard for knowledge?”</h3>
            <p>This is a better objection, and one philosophers have pressed hard. Descartes assumes that genuine knowledge requires absolute certainty — but that assumption is contestable. Most contemporary epistemologists think knowledge does not require certainty, only justified true belief (or something close to it). If Descartes’ standard is too high, his project may be misguided from the start. But notice: even if you reject his standard, you still have to explain where knowledge comes from and what makes it reliable. Descartes is raising a real question, even if his answer is not the only possible one.</p>
            <h3>“Can I really doubt that 2 + 2 = 4?”</h3>
            <p>This is the hardest case for the Evil Demon. Descartes acknowledges that mathematics seems immune to the Dream Argument — but not, he thinks, to the Evil Demon. If a sufficiently powerful deceiver exists, it could make him believe that simple arithmetic is valid when it is not. Many readers find this implausible — it is hard to imagine what it would even mean to doubt that 2 + 2 = 4. But the question is worth sitting with. The fact that something seems undeniable does not by itself prove that it <em>is</em> undeniable. That proof is what Meditation II is for.</p>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-4" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-4" hidden=""><div>
            <p>Meditation I ends at the bottom of the well. Descartes has suspended every belief he can find any reason to doubt. He is left with what seems to be nothing — a mind emptied of certainties, facing a world it can no longer be sure exists.</p>
            <p>Meditation II begins the climb back. As you read it, watch for the moment when the doubt itself becomes evidence of something. If there is a deceiver powerful enough to make him doubt everything — including his own existence — what does that actually imply about <em>him</em>? Notice the exact form of the argument when it arrives. Is it a deduction? An inference? Or something more direct than either?</p>
            
            <div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Looking ahead → Meditation II</p>
                <p>The Evil Demon is the most powerful doubt-inducing tool Descartes has. If anything survives it, that thing will be the foundation he has been looking for. The <em>cogito</em> — <em>I think, therefore I am</em> — is Descartes’ answer. But is it a logical inference, or something more immediate? That question matters more than it might first appear.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div></div>
    
    
    
    
